 { Let's start with something very simple. Black's task is to prevent 2.Ke6, creating the threat of 3.Rh8. The only solution is a rear attack with the rook on White's king. } 1... { -KEY- } Ra1 $1 ( { Equally possible is } 1... Ra2 { , } Ra3 { or even } Ra4 { , but usually in the endgame, the further we can place our rook from the enemy forces, the better. The edges of the board (a- and h-files, 1st and 8th ranks) tend to be the rook's preferred working areas. } ) 
2. Ke6 Re1+ 3. Kd5 Rd1+ ( { Since White isn't actually threatening anything, most other moves do as well; however, leaving the rear angle with } 3... Re8 $2 { would lead to defeat after } 4. Kc6 Rg8 5. Ra7 $1 { Such a passive defence, with king and rook on the back rank, only works against rook's pawns and knight's pawns. } ) 
4. Kc6 Rc1+ { White can't make progress: draw. } 
