 { Now let's see what happens if the weaker side omits the first step in Philidor's defence. } 1... Rb1 ( { Black's first move is actually not the best choice, but I think it is very useful for understanding the topic. As we already know, } 1... Rb6 { is the optimum choice here. } ) 
2. { -KEY- } Kf6 $1 ( { a) Obviously, } 2. Ke6 Rb6 { is useless for White. } ) 
 ( { b) As is } 2. e6 Rf1 ) 
Re1 $1 { Tying the white king to the pawn. } ( { a) In the previous example we saw the successful usage of waiting moves. Here such an idea is clearly mistaken: after } 2... Rb2 $2 3. Ra8 Kd7 4. e6 Kd6 5. Rd8 Kc7 6. e7 { White wins; } ) 
 ( { b) and } 2... Rb6 $2 3. e6 { is equally bad. } ) 
 ( { c) } 2... Rf1 $2 { also loses: } 3. Ke6 Kf8 ( { c1) or } 3... Kd8 4. Ra8 Kc7 5. Ke7 { followed by e5-e6, and the black rook lacks the space for a successful side attack from the h-file. } ) 
4. Ra8 Kg7 5. Kd7 Rd1 ( { the threat was 6.e6, and } 5... Rf7 6. Kd6 { makes no difference; } ) 
6. Ke7 Rb1 7. e6 { and White wins, thanks to his rook controlling the edge (the a-file); e.g. } Rb7 8. Kd6 Rb6 9. Kd7 Rb7 10. Kc6 Re7 11. Kd6 ) 
3. Ke6 ( { Or } 3. e6 Rf1 { in Philidor style. } ) 
Kf8 $1 { To the short side of the pawn, so that the rook will have more space for a future side attack. } ( { Actually } 3... Kd8 { also draws after } 4. Ra8 Kc7 5. Re8 Rh1 6. Kf7 Rh7 7. Kg6 Kd7 $1 { an idea discovered by Lasker; but I wouldn't recommend it since it doesn't work against c- or f-pawns. } ) 
4. Ra8+ Kg7 5. Re8 ( { Or } 5. Kd6 Kf7 $1 { preventing e5-e6. The text creates the threat of 6.Kd7, so Black needs to find a good defence. } ) 
Ra1 $1 { A key move, preparing a side attack now that the white rook left the edge. In fact several other moves such as 5...Re2 would also have worked, using the same method somewhat later. } ( { For example, } 5... Re2 6. Kd7 Ra2 7. e6 Ra7 { with similar play as in the main line. However, in general, I think it makes sense to launch the correct plans as soon as we can. For instance, if we imagine the white king on e7 and the pawn on e6 instead, then ...Ra1 would indeed be mandatory. } ) 
6. Kd7 ( { After } 6. Rd8 Re1 $1 7. Kd6 Kf7 { White hasn't gotten anywhere. } ) 
Ra7+ 7. Kd6 Ra6+ { Draw. This kind of defensive set-up, with the king on the short side and the rook on the long side of the pawn, is referred to as 'the defence of the long side' in this book. } 
