5. Ra1 $1 { (a switch of angle with the aim of dislodging the enemy king from the g-file, thus liberating squares for his own king) } Rb7 6. Kd6 Rb6 7. Kd7 Rb7 8. Kd8 Kf6 ( { c2) or } 8. Rd6 Rb8 9. Kc7 Rb2 10. Re1 $1 { and the pawn reaches e7, after which the bridge decides; } ) 
9. e7 Rb8 10. Kc7 Re8 11. Kd6 $1 Rb8 12. Rf1 Kg7 13. Kc7 Ra8 14. Ra1 $1 Re8 15. Kd7 { and wins. This variation shows the importance of controlling the edge with the rook, in this case the a-file. } ) 
